{
  "description": "The Legend rows Python's renderer draws by itself (gbdraw/legend/table.py::_generated_legend_rows) for some legend inputs, each with the palette key of its fill ('' when no palette key colors it, as for Depth). Python's answer is the keys, in order (tests/test_legend_row_facts.py); the Web app's is the palette key it reads from each key (services/specific-color-rules.js::legendRowType), with every key a track row or a row of a present feature type (tests/web/legend-feature-rows.test.mjs). `used_color_rules` are the captioned rules the drawn features take, each as [the features' type, caption, color] (gbdraw/features/colors.py::legend_rule_use). The Web app leaves these rows out of a rule's rows (OV-307) and colors them from the palette live (candidate-render.js::draftLegendRowColors). `feature_rows` pins the feature rows of a Legend and the row of each drawn feature (gbdraw/legend/table.py::FeatureLegendRows, the Web's twin services/specific-color-rules.js::featureLegendRows). `rules` are color table rows [feature type or '*', caption, color]; `drawn` are the drawn features, each [type, the index in `rules` of the rule it takes, or null for no rule]. The answer: `feature_rows`, the row of each drawn feature (a type's own or \"other\" row by its key; a captioned rule's row as [caption, color], whose key tests/fixtures/legend_rule_keys.json allocates; null for none); `type_rows`, the renderer row of each drawn type (null for none); `rows`, the feature rows in Legend order (a rule row by the index of its rule). A rule counts as used for a type only when features of that type take it (OV-3465). tests/test_legend_row_facts.py and tests/web/legend-feature-rows.test.mjs read them.",
